# Why rain is fresh
subtitle: "How the sun distils salty seawater into fresh rain, and why the very same cycle has slowly made the sea salty."
thumb: notebook

## Why rain is fresh
use: water_cycle
with:
    stages:
      - evaporation
      - condensation
      - precipitation
      - runoff
      - infiltration
    labels: false
    groundwater: true
    precipType: rain
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 150
      w: 1000
      h: 580
sources:
    - id: trenberth2007
      title: Estimates of the global water budget and its annual cycle using observational and model data
      authors: Trenberth KE, Smith L, Qian T, Dai A, Fasullo JT
      year: 2007
      finding: Ocean evaporation about 413 000 km³ a year; net transport of about 40 000 km³ a year from ocean to land, returned by runoff.

    text salt_stays "salt stays" #7a5c2e x 194 y 274 font_size 31 hidden true
    text salt_in "+ salt" #7a5c2e x 450 y 400 font_size 31 hidden true
    text fresh "fresh" left rain gap 20 #2f6b8f font_size 32 hidden true
    text c_evap "400,000 km³" #6a4c93 x 162 y 483 font_size 31 hidden true
    text c_return "40,000 km³" #2f6b8f x 795 y 430 font_size 29 hidden true
    text c_wt "water table" #2f6b8f x 828 y 340 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Why rain is fresh"

Most rain starts in the salty sea, yet it falls fresh. Why? To find out, follow one drop round the water cycle, from the ocean to the mountain and back.
    draw ocean_fill ocean_surface land_fill land_surface bedrock bedrock_hatch

The engine is the sun. Its warmth pulls water off the sea surface as invisible vapour.
    draw sun sun_rays

That is evaporation, and only the water goes. The salt cannot evaporate, so it stays behind in the sea.
    draw evaporation salt_stays

So the sun works like a still the size of the oceans: over four hundred thousand cubic kilometres of fresh water rise off them each year.
    draw c_evap

    pause 0.3

Rising air cools, and the vapour condenses on specks of dust as tiny droplets: a cloud.
    draw cloud_small cloud_small_fill

Wind carries it inland, over the high ground, where the droplets merge and grow.
    draw condensation cloud_main cloud_main_fill

Once they are too heavy for the rising air to hold, they fall as fresh rain: precipitation. A water molecule stays up there only about nine days.
    draw rain fresh

On the slope the rain gathers into streams and then a river, running back toward the sea.
    draw runoff river

Some soaks into the ground instead, filling the pores of the rock below the water table.
    draw infiltration water_table aquifer_fill c_wt

That groundwater creeps seaward too, over years or even centuries.
    draw gw_flow

Together they return about forty thousand cubic kilometres a year: the surplus the wind carried in over the land.
    draw c_return

But on the way, rivers pick up a little salt, dissolved out of the rocks.
    draw salt_in

Evaporation never lifts it back, so over the ages the sea grew salty.
    highlight salt_stays salt_in

So one cycle does both jobs: it hands the land fresh water, and it leaves the sea its salt.
    point at.ref river gesture circle

    quiz "The Dead Sea has rivers flowing in but none flowing out. Why does it keep getting saltier?"
        right: Rivers bring a little salt in, and water leaves only by evaporating, so the salt piles up.
        + Water leaves only by evaporating, and the salt stays
        * The rivers bring in seawater
        * The sun makes new salt
---
